# Rank 1902 / CVE-2023-27591: Miniflux gated the optional /metrics endpoint
# on a CIDR allowlist whose tested address came from the client-IP helper
# rather than the transport peer (CWE-287 / CWE-348):
#
#   func isAllowedToAccessMetricsEndpoint(r *http.Request) bool {
#       clientIP := request.ClientIP(r)
#       ...
#       for _, cidr := range config.Opts.MetricsAllowedNetworks() {
#           _, network, err := net.ParseCIDR(cidr)
#           ...
#           if network.Contains(net.ParseIP(clientIP)) {
#               return true
#           }
#       }
#       return false
#   }
#
# `request.ClientIP` reads a value the middleware stored from
# `request.FindClientIP`, which returns the first entry of `X-Forwarded-For`
# (or `X-Real-IP`) before ever consulting `r.RemoteAddr` -- and a proxy
# *appends* to X-Forwarded-For, so the leading entry is whatever the client
# sent. Any request carrying `X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1` reads back as
# inside the allowed network and reaches the metrics endpoint, no credential
# involved. Fix commit b46b5dfb2aa3a1f5320a7a3420b9186bb8fc655f routes the
# membership test through the new `request.FindRemoteIP(r)` (RemoteAddr
# only, with the recommendation to use the metrics HTTP basic auth instead)
# and demotes the header-preferring helper to log lines. `FindClientIP`
# itself prefers the headers -- so the
# detection must sit at the decision, not at the parse.
#
# FIXED. bindings/go/forwarded_header_client_ip_allowlist.vyql binds
# forwardedHeaderClientIpAllowlist: a function-scoped presence match on a
# `net.ParseCIDR` call plus a `net.ParseIP` argument that flows from a call
# whose method is one of the client-IP spellings (ClientIP / GetClientIP /
# FindClientIP / RealIP and their capitalisations -- the helper names gin,
# echo and the X-Forwarded-For documentation themselves use for the
# header-preferring lookup). It reuses code.ProxyClientIpSpoofingBypass
# (VYQL-AUTH-012), shipped for the CasaOS gateway hop, the Hono ALB
# conn-info read and the Python leftmost-split recipe -- the same weakness
# read at the allowlist that consumes the helper instead of at the header
# parse. No new concept, no new rule. The flow tie is the discriminator: at
# the parent the `net.ParseIP` node's argument carries the helper's method
# token; at the fixed revision the argument is the peer-address call inline and the
# token changes, so the negation never names the fixed revision's helper. A
# proxy-aware helper is indistinguishable at the call site, which is why
# the issue is a medium-confidence review flag.
#
# Measured over three `--cache off` runs per revision of the full tree:
# 176 findings (37 at or above high) with exactly 1 VYQL-AUTH-012 at
# b46b5dfb^ on service/httpd/httpd.go:224
# (isAllowedToAccessMetricsEndpoint) and 175 findings (36 high) with 0 at
# b46b5dfb; no other rule count moved. Reachable-set enumeration over
# the 87 corpus clones: exactly two Go files call net.ParseCIDR (this one
# and traefik's pkg/ip/checker.go, which parses static masks and carries no
# client-IP call), and no other file combines net.ParseIP with any
# client-IP spelling, so there is no collateral to measure beyond traefik's
# clean zero.

test "rank1902: the metrics CIDR allowlist tests the client-IP helper's result"
  lang go
  file service/httpd/httpd.go
  expect VYQL-AUTH-012
  expect_review code.ProxyClientIpSpoofingBypass
  code
  ```
  package httpd

  func isAllowedToAccessMetricsEndpoint(r *http.Request) bool {
      clientIP := request.ClientIP(r)

      for _, cidr := range config.Opts.MetricsAllowedNetworks() {
          _, network, err := net.ParseCIDR(cidr)
          if err != nil {
              logger.Fatal(`[Metrics] Unable to parse CIDR %v`, err)
          }

          if network.Contains(net.ParseIP(clientIP)) {
              return true
          }
      }

      return false
  }
  ```

